\title{The Division Rule}
\date{2026-04-29T16:36:21Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Principle}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Suppose we have #{n} total choices, but we really want to think of some of them as being equivalent. If the choices always come in groups of exactly #{k} choices which are all equivalent (\em{i.e.} each [equivalence class](0098) is of size exactly #{k}), then the number of “really different” choices (the number of equivalence classes) is #{n/k}.}
